# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is git-ignored and must never be committed.
#
# The Makefile loads this file and exports it to every recipe, and passes it on
# to docker compose, so `make up`, `make migrate-up` and `make test-integration`
# need nothing exported by hand. Running a binary directly outside make does:
# `env $(grep -v "^#" .env | xargs -d "\n") go run ./cmd/api`, or export the
# variables yourself.
#
# Do not `source .env`. The DSN below contains `(`, `)` and `&`, which bash
# parses rather than takes literally, and sourcing sets shell variables that a
# child process does not inherit anyway. For the same reason the values here
# stay unquoted: make and compose both parse this file directly, and quotes
# would become part of the value.

# ---- Chat LLM (any OpenAI-compatible endpoint) -----------------------------
# Default is DeepSeek. Pointing these at any other OpenAI-compatible provider
# is all it takes to switch backends — the agent runtime knows nothing about
# the provider, and a smoke test proves it.
LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com/v1
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_MODEL=deepseek-chat

# ---- Embeddings (separate endpoint, separate key) --------------------------
# DeepSeek has no embeddings endpoint, so this is a different provider.
# SiliconFlow hosts the same BAAI/bge-m3 the Elasticsearch mapping is built
# for, at 1024 dimensions. Changing the model here means reindexing everything.
EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=https://api.siliconflow.cn/v1
EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
EMBEDDING_MODEL=BAAI/bge-m3

# How many texts go in one request, how long one request may take, and how many
# times it may be retried after its first attempt. A document of 2000 chunks is
# 63 requests at the default batch size, which is why the handler has a deadline
# of its own; see INGEST_DOCUMENT_TIMEOUT.
EMBED_BATCH_SIZE=32
EMBED_TIMEOUT=30s
EMBED_MAX_RETRIES=3

# ---- Elasticsearch --------------------------------------------------------
# ES_PORT is what docker compose publishes; ELASTICSEARCH_URL is what the Go
# binaries dial, and the two have to agree the way MYSQL_PORT and MYSQL_DSN do.
# Inside the compose network the address is http://elasticsearch:9200 instead.
#
# Every read and write goes through the alias, not the concrete index behind it.
# That is what makes changing the embedding model survivable: chunks_v2 can be
# built alongside chunks_v1 and the alias switched atomically.
ES_PORT=9200
ELASTICSEARCH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9200
ES_INDEX_ALIAS=chunks

# Like TEST_MYSQL_DSN: the Elasticsearch integration tests skip without this,
# and `make test-integration` refuses to run rather than reporting a green pass
# in which every one of them was skipped.
TEST_ELASTICSEARCH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9200

# ---- MySQL ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The first five are read by docker compose when it starts the server; the DSN
# is read by the Go binaries. They have to agree — in particular MYSQL_PORT and
# the port inside MYSQL_DSN, which is what a second MySQL already listening on
# 3306 will otherwise quietly break.
#
# parseTime=true&loc=UTC are not optional. Without parseTime the driver returns
# DATETIME(6) as bytes and every scan into a time.Time fails; without loc=UTC it
# interprets stored timestamps in the local zone. The store refuses to start if
# either is missing.
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root
MYSQL_DATABASE=incident_diag
MYSQL_USER=incident
MYSQL_PASSWORD=incident
MYSQL_PORT=3307
MYSQL_DSN=incident:incident@tcp(127.0.0.1:3307)/incident_diag?parseTime=true&loc=UTC

# Connection pool. Defaults shown.
DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=25
DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=25
DB_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=5m

# Integration tests run only when this is set, so `go test ./...` stays green
# on a machine with no infrastructure. They create and drop schema, so this
# must not point at a database holding anything you want to keep.
TEST_MYSQL_DSN=incident:incident@tcp(127.0.0.1:3307)/incident_diag?parseTime=true&loc=UTC

# ---- Kafka ----------------------------------------------------------------
# KAFKA_PORT is what docker compose publishes and what the broker advertises to
# clients outside its network; KAFKA_BROKERS is what the Go binaries dial. They
# have to agree, the same way MYSQL_PORT and MYSQL_DSN do. Inside the compose
# network the address is kafka:9092 instead.
KAFKA_PORT=9094
KAFKA_BROKERS=127.0.0.1:9094

# How long a produce may take, acknowledgement included. This is spent inside
# the upload request: with the broker down, an upload blocks and then answers
# 201 anyway, because the document was created and the reconciler will enqueue
# it.
#
# This value is NOT the upper bound on that block. franz-go's dial backoff and
# metadata refresh sit around the produce rather than inside it, so a 10s
# setting measured 13.5-16.7s with the broker stopped. Keep this at or below
# HALF of HTTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT, or the client is cut off before it gets the 201.
KAFKA_PRODUCE_TIMEOUT=10s

# Like TEST_MYSQL_DSN: the Kafka integration tests skip without this, and
# `make test-integration` refuses to run rather than reporting a green pass in
# which every one of them was skipped.
TEST_KAFKA_BROKERS=127.0.0.1:9094

# ---- Ingestion and the reconciler -----------------------------------------
# INGEST_LEASE must exceed the longest legitimate ingestion, or a slow document
# gets claimed a second time while the first attempt is still working on it.
# The reconciler uses the same value to decide a claim was abandoned.
INGEST_LEASE=10m
INGEST_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# The deadline on one document, applied the moment the claim succeeds and
# covering everything from reading the file to the terminal write. Without it
# the worst case is not bounded at all: 2000 chunks of embedding requests, each
# retried with backoff, can run for over an hour.
#
# It must stay below INGEST_LEASE, or a slow document is claimed a second time
# while the first attempt is still working on it. The worker refuses to start
# otherwise. The consumer's rebalance timeout is derived from this value rather
# than configured separately, so that constraint cannot be broken by raising
# this one.
INGEST_DOCUMENT_TIMEOUT=5m

# The sweep that re-enqueues rows nothing will ever send a message for again.
# RECONCILE_BATCH is per category, and it is what stops a backlog that built up
# while Kafka was down from coming back as one thundering herd.
RECONCILE_INTERVAL=30s
RECONCILE_PENDING_AFTER=60s
RECONCILE_BATCH=100

# ---- HTTP server ----------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults shown. The SSE endpoint in M26 will need its own handling rather
# than a write timeout raised until it stops mattering.
HTTP_ADDR=:8080
HTTP_READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT=5s
HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT=30s
HTTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s
HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT=120s
HTTP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=15s

# ---- Uploaded documents ---------------------------------------------------
# The API writes these files and the ingestion worker reads them, so both must
# point at the same volume. The default root is a container path; running the
# binaries directly wants something writable, such as ./data/documents.
DOCUMENT_STORAGE_ROOT=/var/lib/incident_diag/documents
DOCUMENT_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=10485760

# Chunking. The target is a budget rather than a ceiling: a single paragraph,
# list item, table or fenced code block larger than this becomes an oversized
# chunk rather than being split. It sits far below the embedding model's 8192,
# so an estimate wrong by a factor of two still fits.
#
# The cap is what stops one pathological upload from consuming the whole
# embedding budget; a document over it fails without embedding anything.
CHUNK_TARGET_TOKENS=400
CHUNK_MAX_PER_DOCUMENT=2000

# ---- Incident Lab ---------------------------------------------------------
# The two demo services, Prometheus, Grafana and cAdvisor run behind compose's
# `lab` profile: `make up-lab` starts them, `make up` does not. Integration
# tests need none of them.
#
# The defaults come from the knowledge corpus in testdata/knowledge, and the
# scenarios in `make scenario` are tuned against them. PAYMENT_POOL_SIZE is the
# March 2026 postmortem's value; CHECKOUT_PAYMENT_TIMEOUT is the May 2026 one,
# before someone lowered it below payment-service's 800ms p99.
#
# Demand on the pool is the request rate multiplied by how long a charge takes,
# so raising the processor latency without raising the pool is what saturates
# it. See docs/plans/observability-and-fault-scenarios.md.
PAYMENT_POOL_SIZE=20
PAYMENT_PROCESSOR_LATENCY_MS=50
CHECKOUT_PAYMENT_TIMEOUT=2s

# Only used when a lab service is run directly rather than through compose:
# the compose file sets both itself, to the container-internal names and paths.
# CHECKOUT_PAYMENT_URL has no default — a checkout-service quietly calling
# localhost would look healthy while charging nothing — and LAB_LOG_DIR
# defaults to /var/log/lab, which a developer's user cannot create.
CHECKOUT_PAYMENT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8082
LAB_LOG_DIR=data/lab-logs

# Published ports. The services listen on 8080 inside their containers.
CHECKOUT_PORT=8081
PAYMENT_PORT=8082
PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
GRAFANA_PORT=3000
CADVISOR_PORT=8083
GRAFANA_PASSWORD=admin

# The lab services write <service>.log into ./data/lab-logs, which ops-mcp will
# mount read-only. They run as this user because that directory belongs to
# whoever ran make; the Makefile exports both from `id` unless they are set
# here.
# LAB_UID=1000
# LAB_GID=1000
